# CareerGuide AI — backend configuration
# Copy this file to `backend/.env`. Every value below has a working default,
# so the app runs with no .env at all. Never commit a real .env.

# --- Database ------------------------------------------------------------------
# The name printed at the top of pages and PDFs. Almost never worth changing.
APP_NAME=CareerGuide AI

# SQLite by default: one file, no service to run, nothing to configure.
DATABASE_URL=sqlite:///./careerguide.db

# To use a hosted PostgreSQL instead — Neon, Supabase, or your own server — replace
# the line above. Supabase: Dashboard -> Project settings -> Database -> Connection
# string, and pick either form; Neon: Dashboard -> Connection string, and "pooled"
# is the one that survives a traffic spike:
#
#   session pooler / direct, port 5432     (keeps prepared statements)
#   transaction pooler, port 6543          (what the free tier scales on)
#
# DATABASE_URL=postgresql+psycopg://postgres.<project-ref>:<password>@<host>:5432/postgres
# DATABASE_URL=postgresql://neondb_owner:<password>@<endpoint>.pooler.<region>.aws.neon.tech/<db>
#
# Either form as those dashboards print it is accepted: a plain `postgres://` or
# `postgresql://` prefix is rewritten to `+psycopg`, the driver in requirements.txt,
# so you do not have to know the driver name to paste a URL. `sslmode=require` is
# added too when the host is not this machine and the URL says nothing about TLS —
# Neon and Supabase both terminate TLS at the pooler and neither writes the parameter
# into the string it hands you. A URL that already names a mode is left exactly as
# written, including `sslmode=disable`: that is somebody's decision, not an omission.
#
# +psycopg on the line yourself is still fine and is not rewritten twice. If the
# password contains @ : / or %, URL-encode it (%40, %3A, %2F, %25) or the URL will
# not parse — and an unparseable URL is left alone apart from the driver, so the
# connection fails loudly rather than going out unencrypted by guesswork.
# Both Supabase pooler modes work as shipped: the engine recycles connections and
# skips server-side prepared statements. See the note in app/database.py.
#
# The first boot against an empty database creates every table and seeds the
# career catalogue. Turn that off once the database is ready, which is what you
# want on serverless hosting where a cold start should not run DDL:
#   .venv/Scripts/python.exe scripts/db_init.py     # prepare it once
#   DB_AUTO_SETUP=false                             # afterwards
#
# Check where you stand with:
#   .venv/Scripts/python.exe scripts/db_check.py
#
# On a host that rebuilds the container on every push (Render, Fly, a plain
# container platform), say so with REQUIRE_POSTGRES=true. A SQLite URL there would
# accept a child's account and lose it at the next deploy, so the app boots, answers
# /api/health and refuses every data route with a sentence naming DATABASE_URL
# instead of writing to a disk that is about to disappear. Vercel is recognised by
# its own environment variable and needs no setting here.
REQUIRE_POSTGRES=false

# A real URL contains the database password. Keep it in .env only — never in
# .env.example, never in source, never in a commit, never pasted into chat.
DB_AUTO_SETUP=true

# The browser this API answers to. One origin — scheme, host, port, no trailing
# slash, no path — and it is the address the site is actually opened at. This is the
# setting a deployment gets wrong, and it fails quietly: with it unset the app falls
# back to the three development origins below and logs a warning naming each one, so
# a laptop keeps working while every request from the internet is refused by the
# browser before it reaches this API.
#
#   FRONTEND_URL=http://localhost:3000                 # local development
#   FRONTEND_URL=https://careerguide.example           # deployed
#
# Several frontends on one API: a comma-separated list is accepted.
# A `*` is never honoured anywhere in this file — a credentialed any-origin API is
# the same hole as no API, and this one answers with children's conversations.
FRONTEND_URL=http://localhost:3000

# Origins *beyond* FRONTEND_URL, and nothing else. The list is the one above plus
# this one, so adding an entry here cannot lock out the site itself. Its real use is
# school and counsellor portals, which are served from their own subdomains and
# would otherwise sign nobody in — see the portals section further down.
# Blank by default: on a laptop the fallback above already covers the dev server,
# `127.0.0.1` and the friendly `careerguide.localhost` name that Chrome and Edge
# resolve here without editing hosts.
CORS_ORIGINS=

# --- AI provider ----------------------------------------------------------------
# AI_PROVIDER=demo uses the built-in scripted guide, needs no key, and marks every
# reply as demo-generated inside the app so nobody mistakes it for a real model.
# Any OpenAI-compatible provider works: set the three values below.
AI_PROVIDER=demo
AI_API_KEY=
AI_MODEL=
AI_BASE_URL=https://api.openai.com/v1

# --- Voice responses ------------------------------------------------------------
# Three transports, all online, all sent the reply's text to make audio:
#   TTS_PROVIDER=auto    Gemini when GEMINI_API_KEY is set, else Azure when its key
#                        is set, else the free Edge read-aloud endpoint. Edge
#                        answers 403 to datacenter egress IPs, so a deployed site
#                        needs a Gemini or Azure key to speak at all.
#   TTS_PROVIDER=gemini  force Google Gemini (also accepts "google").
#   TTS_PROVIDER=edge    force Edge.
#   TTS_PROVIDER=azure   force Azure. Without a key every spoken reply then fails
#                        with a clear message instead of quietly trying Edge — that
#                        is deliberate, so a broken setup is visible.
# A name matching none of these is logged and treated as auto, so a typo cannot
# silently change which company receives a child's replies.
TTS_PROVIDER=auto

# Gemini TTS. The model name below is what this app was built against; Google
# renames these often, so check what the key can reach before blaming the app when
# speech stops. One voice reads Hindi, English and Hinglish by following the
# script of the text it is given.
# This key is a credential: .env only, never in a commit, never in frontend code,
# never pasted into chat.
GEMINI_API_KEY=
GEMINI_TTS_MODEL=gemini-3.8-flash-tts
GEMINI_VOICE=Sulafat

# Azure Speech: the same Microsoft neural voices Edge uses.
# The key is a credential: .env only, never in a commit, never in frontend code,
# never pasted into chat. On a hosted deployment it belongs in the server's
# environment variables.
AZURE_SPEECH_KEY=
# The Speech resource's own region, e.g. eastus. The key only works in that region.
AZURE_SPEECH_REGION=
TTS_VOICE_HI=hi-IN-SwaraNeural
TTS_VOICE_EN=en-IN-NeerjaNeural
TTS_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=25

# --- Rate limiting --------------------------------------------------------------
# Per-session ceilings, so one browser cannot hammer a paid model or keep a
# questionnaire open forever. Every rule is a `limits` expression —
# "<n> per <second|minute|hour|day>" — and a value that does not parse is refused
# at startup rather than silently switching the guard off.
#
# The bucket is the caller's session — its bearer token if the request wrote one, its
# session cookie if the browser attached one (see the module docstring in
# app/middleware/rate_limit.py). On a shared school IP an address bucket would throttle a
# whole classroom for one enthusiastic student. A call with no session yet falls back to
# the address — which is why the guest lookup gets more per minute than a password route.
#
# Counts live in this process. On serverless hosting every instance keeps its own,
# so these are a floor rather than a global limit; the real defence on a come-back
# code is the password hashing cost, not this counter.
RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED=true
# Every GET that has no rule of its own. Reading your own rows is the cheap half of the
# API, so this is a brake on a scraper that has got hold of a session rather than on a
# student: sixty a minute is one screen a second. The routes a page load or a server
# render depends on, and the ones that answer the same for a stranger as for the family,
# are exempt — the list and the reason for each entry is `_READ_EXEMPT` in
# app/middleware/rate_limit.py.
RATE_LIMIT_READ=60 per minute
# POST /api/conversations and POST /api/conversations/{id}/messages
RATE_LIMIT_CHAT=20 per minute
# POST /api/voice/speak — one turn can produce several sentences
RATE_LIMIT_SPEAK=20 per minute
# POST /api/assessments/{type}/start and .../session/{id}/submit
RATE_LIMIT_ASSESSMENT=10 per minute
# POST /api/assessments/session/{id}/answer autosaves every answer, and the longest
# questionnaire has 30 items. Ten a minute would cut a fast student off mid-test.
RATE_LIMIT_ANSWER=40 per minute
# POST /api/auth/onboarding, /login, /link-child and both /api/auth/clerk/* routes
RATE_LIMIT_AUTH=5 per minute
# POST /api/auth/guest runs on page load for a returning device
RATE_LIMIT_GUEST=30 per minute
# The three routes under /api/payments that talk to Razorpay: create-order, verify and
# downgrade. Six a minute leaves room for a family whose card was declined to try again
# without waiting, and is nothing like the rate at which anyone would enjoy hammering a
# route that calls a payment provider.
RATE_LIMIT_PAYMENT=6 per minute
# The routes under /api/email: attach an address, confirm one, ask for a copy of a
# result, invite a guardian. Each one can cause a call to a paid email service, so
# they get their own bucket rather than riding on RATE_LIMIT_AUTH.
RATE_LIMIT_EMAIL=10 per minute
# POST /api/study-abroad/leads — the one public form that stores contact details. The
# bucket is an hour rather than a minute because a school office legitimately sends a
# handful of enquiries in a morning, and slow enough that a bot fills the table with
# noise rather than with names.
RATE_LIMIT_LEAD=10 per hour
# The routes under /api/admin, /api/exams/admin and the study-abroad admin panel that
# CHANGE something: an account's operator flag, an account's state, a career entry, an
# exam row, a questionnaire item, an enquiry's stage. Every one of those is a thing a
# stolen session could do to a live site, so this bucket decides how fast that could
# happen. The admin *reads* are not given a second ceiling of their own — they fall into
# RATE_LIMIT_READ, which at sixty a minute is not something an operator reloading a
# dashboard reaches.
RATE_LIMIT_ADMIN=10 per minute
# POST /api/ai/resume-suggest, the one resume route that spends an outside call. Saving a
# resume needs no bucket of its own: it writes the one row the student is looking at, and
# a student who saves twice in a minute has pressed a button twice.
RATE_LIMIT_RESUME=10 per minute
# POST /api/assessments/result/{id}/roadmap — the ✨ button that rewrites a finished
# result's four-week plan with the model instead of the app's own rules. One route that
# spends an outside call, like the resume suggestion, but set tighter on purpose: what it
# overwrites is a document a student may have already printed, so a few fast clicks should
# not spin the model three times while they decide. Reading a result needs no bucket of
# its own — the plan arrives with the result, on the ordinary read rule.
RATE_LIMIT_ROADMAP=6 per minute
# POST /api/ai/mock-interview — one call is one turn of a practice interview, so a whole
# sitting is six of them: five questions and the closing. Set per minute rather than per
# interview because the useful thing to stop is a script, not a student: someone who is
# mid-answer must never be the one who pays for this bucket. Nothing is written by this
# route, so a refusal costs a student no work.
RATE_LIMIT_INTERVIEW=12 per minute
# WHERE the counters above are kept. Leave blank for one process — that is the
# default, and it is what every deployment of this app so far runs.
#
# Set it to a Redis URL the moment the API runs as more than one worker or more than
# one container, because a limit held in process memory is a limit per process: four
# workers quietly turn "20 per minute" into 80, and a shared school IP gets a
# different answer depending on which worker its next request lands on. This is the
# only thing in this app that uses Redis — there is no queue, no cache and no session
# store — so a Redis with nothing pointed at it is a service nobody calls.
#   RATE_LIMIT_REDIS_URL=redis://redis:6379/0
# If Redis is unreachable, throttling falls back to this process rather than failing
# the request: a dead guard is worse than a weak one, and a student mid-message does
# not need to learn about your cache. /app/admin/settings prints which of the two you
# are running, so the fallback cannot stay invisible.
RATE_LIMIT_REDIS_URL=
# Set true ONLY when a proxy you control overwrites X-Forwarded-For on every
# request. Trusting a header a caller can set is an invitation to jump your own
# queue.
RATE_LIMIT_TRUST_FORWARDED_FOR=false

# --- Login tokens ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Tokens are random opaque strings; nothing is signed with a secret, so there is no
# key to guard here.
#
# What the database keeps is `sha256(token)`, not the token. The gap that used to be
# recorded in this file — that `auth_tokens` held each bearer string exactly as issued,
# so a dump of it was a list of live sessions — is closed, and
# `0010_hashed_device_and_token` converted the sessions that were already live. The
# same applies to the device id `/api/auth/guest` accepts.
#
# A session lasts a day and renews itself while the page is in use, up to a 30-day
# ceiling counted from the sign-in that minted it. The number is small on purpose: a
# token copied off a shared laptop is worth a day of a child's account, not a month.
SESSION_TOKEN_TTL_HOURS=24
SESSION_ABSOLUTE_TTL_HOURS=720

# --- The session cookie ---------------------------------------------------------
# Every response that starts a session — onboarding, `/api/auth/guest`, a code login, a
# Clerk entry — also writes this cookie, and `Authorization: Bearer` still works beside
# it. The browser sends the cookie; no page script can read it; the database holds only
# its digest, so nothing here is a secret to keep and there is no key to guard.
AUTH_COOKIE_NAME=cg_session
# How the cookie is marked. `auto` reads the request: behind a proxy that says
# `X-Forwarded-Proto: https` it writes `Secure`, and `http://localhost` /
# `http://*.localhost` are trusted without it, because that is where a browser's own
# rules already make an insecure `SameSite=None` cookie impossible to keep. Force `true`
# on a deployment that terminates TLS somewhere this app cannot see, and `false` only
# for a test server with no certificate at all.
#
# When `Secure` cannot be honoured, `SameSite=None` is downgraded to `Lax` rather than
# sent in a form the browser would silently drop.
AUTH_COOKIE_SECURE=auto
AUTH_COOKIE_SAMESITE=none
# `none` is what a page on one host calling an API on another needs, and it is also what
# Safari and Firefox-with-strict-protection refuse to store. Those browsers fall back to
# the bearer header the frontend holds in memory for the length of one tab — never in
# localStorage, never in a cookie a script can reach — which is why `token` is still in
# the login responses at all. Put the API under the page's own host (a reverse proxy
# routing /api to it, or Vercel rewrites) and this whole problem goes away: the cookie
# then carries every request by itself and survives a reload.

# --- Clerk (the optional email sign-in) -----------------------------------------
# All three are blank by default, and blank is a supported state, not a broken one:
# the app keeps asking its one question, this browser stays the account, and the
# four routes under /api/auth/clerk answer 503 naming what is missing. Nothing here
# is needed for the app to work, and no Clerk account has to exist for anyone using
# it today.
#
# What it is for: a student or guardian who wants to open the same work from a
# different phone. Clerk then holds the email and the password; this database holds
# only Clerk's user id (users.clerk_user_id) — see the comment in app/models.py.
#
#   CLERK_SECRET_KEY                 sk_test_… or sk_live_… from Clerk -> API keys.
#                                    Server-side only. Used to check a session token
#                                    with Clerk; never sent to the browser.
#   CLERK_API_BASE                   Leave as-is unless Clerk is self-hosted or a
#                                    custom domain is configured.
#   CLERK_WEBHOOK_SIGNING_SECRET     whsec_… from Clerk -> Webhooks -> the
#                                    /api/webhooks/clerk endpoint you add there.
#                                    Without it every delivery is refused, which is
#                                    the safe direction: an unsigned body could
#                                    otherwise disconnect someone's account.
#
# The matching browser-side key (NEXT_PUBLIC_CLERK_PUBLISHABLE_KEY) belongs in
# frontend/.env.local — it is not a secret, but it does not belong in this file.
# A real key is a secret. Keep it in .env only, never in a commit, never pasted
# into chat.
CLERK_SECRET_KEY=
CLERK_API_BASE=https://api.clerk.com
CLERK_WEBHOOK_SIGNING_SECRET=

# --- Email ------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Resend, for the six messages a student or a parent can be sent: a welcome, a
# questionnaire result, a weekly summary, a payment receipt, an address confirmation
# and a parent's invitation. Both values below are blank, and blank is a supported
# state — the same shape the Clerk and Razorpay settings use. With no key:
#   * nothing in this app contacts Resend,
#   * a route that could have mailed reports `not-configured` honestly, so the
#     screen says email is not enabled on this deployment instead of promising a
#     message nobody will receive,
#   * the outbox in `email_jobs` still records a row for the sends that a later
#     boot can deliver, which is what makes a key added tomorrow useful.
#
#   RESEND_API_KEY       re_… from Resend -> API keys. A credential: it can mail
#                        any address in the world, so it stays on the server. Never
#                        in a commit, never in frontend code, never pasted into chat.
#   FROM_EMAIL           the one sender address this service is allowed to use, and
#                        it has to be a domain verified in Resend or every message
#                        lands in spam. A route cannot choose its own sender: that
#                        would be a way to make a mail that looks like ours.
#   EMAIL_REPLY_TO       optional. Where a family's reply lands. Left blank until a
#                        real mailbox exists, because a Reply-To nobody reads is
#                        worse than a reply that goes back to the sender.
#   RESEND_API_BASE      the endpoint. It is the real one already; override it only
#                        to point a test at a stub.
#   EMAIL_TIMEOUT_SECONDS  synthesis of the HTTP call; a send that hangs must not
#                        hold a child's conversation turn open.
#
# Retries and staleness are settings for the same reason: an address that has
# refused four attempts should stop being attempted, and a weekly summary that is
# three weeks late is not a summary.
#
# Note there are two keys here in the same company's name. The frontend's
# /api/contact uses NEXT_PUBLIC-free RESEND_API_KEY in *its own* project settings,
# for one inbound enquiry form; this key is for the messages the backend sends
# about a specific account. They are separate deployments, and neither one reads
# the other's environment.
RESEND_API_KEY=
FROM_EMAIL=
EMAIL_REPLY_TO=
RESEND_API_BASE=https://api.resend.com
EMAIL_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=15
EMAIL_JOBS_MAX_ATTEMPTS=4
EMAIL_STALE_AFTER_HOURS=72
# A summary needs a clock and a serverless deployment has none, so the interval is
# measured against the last one sent whenever anything drains the outbox.
EMAIL_DIGEST_INTERVAL_DAYS=7
# Where a study abroad enquiry is sent. Blank is a supported state: the enquiry is
# stored and the admin list is where it is read, and the answer says nobody was
# notified rather than pretending otherwise.
#
# This one is deliberately NOT editable from any screen, including the admin one. A
# public form that could choose the mailbox its own notifications go to is a free
# forwarding service for every family's name and phone number, from any account that
# can reach the settings route. It is read from this file and nowhere else.
LEAD_NOTIFY_EMAIL=

# --- Payments -------------------------------------------------------------------
# Razorpay, so a family can pay for Pro. Everything about money here is switched
# off by the two blanks below — the same shape Clerk uses. With no key,
# GET /api/payments/plans still reports the real prices and limits,
# `payments_enabled` comes back false, and the pricing page disables its own
# Upgrade buttons and says why. No route pretends a payment succeeded.
#
#   RAZORPAY_KEY_ID          rzp_test_… or rzp_live_… . Public by design: it is
#                            sent to the browser (inside the order response and
#                            /api/payments/me) because Razorpay's own checkout
#                            sheet needs it. It names the account; it authorises
#                            nothing. There is no frontend copy of this value —
#                            NEXT_PUBLIC_RAZORPAY_KEY_ID does not exist in this
#                            app, so a key set here reaches the browser on its own.
#   RAZORPAY_KEY_SECRET      A server credential. Never in a bundle, never in a
#                            commit, never pasted into chat. It signs every order
#                            this server creates and is the only thing that can
#                            prove a callback came from Razorpay.
#   RAZORPAY_WEBHOOK_SECRET  The webhook endpoint's own secret (whsec_…), shown
#                            when the endpoint is created in the Razorpay
#                            dashboard. Without it POST /api/payments/webhook
#                            refuses every delivery, which is the safe direction:
#                            an unsigned body would otherwise be a way to grant a
#                            paid plan for nothing.
#   RAZORPAY_API_BASE        The endpoint. It is the real one already; override it
#                            only to point a test suite at a stub.
#
# Whether this deployment is in test mode is read off the key id's prefix rather
# than set by hand, so a sandbox payment can never be recorded as money later.
#
# Then, in the Razorpay dashboard: create a webhook for
#   https://<backend origin>/api/payments/webhook
# subscribed to payment.captured and payment.failed, paste its secret into
# RAZORPAY_WEBHOOK_SECRET above, and watch one real capture in the server log —
# the suite in scripts/payments_test.py stubs the provider, so it can prove the
# signature rules and the ledger but not that a live order opens.
RAZORPAY_KEY_ID=
RAZORPAY_KEY_SECRET=
RAZORPAY_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
RAZORPAY_API_BASE=https://api.razorpay.com/v1

# --- What the free plan is allowed ----------------------------------------------
# The limits the pricing page advertises. These four values are the single source:
# /api/payments/plans serves them to the browser and entitlements.py enforces the
# same objects, so a page that says "3 chats a day" and a limiter that allows 5
# cannot both be true and disagree.
#
# Enforcement is behind a flag that is OFF by default, and that is a decision
# rather than an unfinished thing: every child using this app today was told
# talking to the guide is free, so switching a daily cap on belongs on the day
# pricing actually goes live, not in the merge that added the column. Flip it
# alongside real keys, and re-check the prices in app/services/plans.py.
PLAN_LIMITS_ENFORCED=false
FREE_DAILY_CHATS=3
FREE_ASSESSMENT_TOTAL=1
# A "day" for a student is an Indian day. Rolling the counter at UTC midnight
# would hand a child back their three questions at 5:30 in the morning, before
# they have slept. 330 minutes is IST; it is a setting only because Nepal,
# Bangladesh and a handful of others are not the same number.
QUOTA_UTC_OFFSET_MINUTES=330

# --- School and counsellor portals -------------------------------------------------
# The domain a portal's own address hangs off: `green-school.<TENANT_BASE_DOMAIN>` is
# that school's portal. Locally `*.careerguide.localhost` resolves with no DNS setup, so
# the subdomain paths are testable before a wildcard record exists. A portal also works
# without any hostname, at `/<kind>/dashboard?portal=<slug>` — which is what a fresh
# deployment gets, and what its screens say out loud rather than pretending otherwise.
TENANT_BASE_DOMAIN=careerguide.localhost
# The mirror of RATE_LIMIT_TRUST_FORWARD_FOR, unsafe for the same reason: X-Forwarded-Host
# is a header any client can write, and believing it is how one school's branding gets
# painted onto another school's portal. Set true only behind a proxy that overwrites the
# header (Vercel and Render do). Left false, a spoofed host simply resolves no portal.
TENANT_TRUST_FORWARDED_HOST=false
# How long an unaccepted invitation stays open, in days. Bulk lists are a promise to a
# family that someone at that address was expected; a year later the class has moved on
# and a link that still works is a link that can still be forwarded.
ORG_INVITE_VALID_DAYS=21
# Two buckets, because the two actions are not equally rare. Opening or changing a portal
# itself is the tighter one; inviting is the one where each accepted row can end up
# mailing somebody who never asked.
RATE_LIMIT_ORG=5 per minute
RATE_LIMIT_ORG_INVITE=10 per minute

# Who may see a portal's roster, analytics and notes is decided by the server on every
# request from the membership row, never by the page. Nothing here turns that on or off.
# A student account cannot open a portal at all: a school's portal is opened by whoever
# runs this deployment, and a counsellor's practice by an adult account.

# --- What a printed page says about this site -----------------------------------
# The two addresses a PDF report has to carry: where the app lives, and who to
# write to about a wrong answer. The defaults are the current public URLs, not a
# placeholder domain, so a deployment on another host overrides both — a report
# that tells a family to email an address nobody reads is worse than one that says
# it does not know.
PUBLIC_SITE_URL=https://careerguide-web.vercel.app
PUBLIC_REPO_URL=https://github.com/anand07kuma-spec/careerguide-ai

# --- Deploying to Vercel ----------------------------------------------------------
# Vercel reads these from Project Settings -> Environment Variables (or
# `vercel env add`), never from this file, and this file must not be uploaded
# (see .vercelignore). The backend project's Root Directory is `backend`, and
# Vercel finds the FastAPI `app` in app/main.py on its own.
#
#   DATABASE_URL       required. Postgres (Supabase). The app refuses to start
#                      on Vercel with a SQLite URL, because that data would
#                      vanish between instances.
#   DB_AUTO_SETUP      false, after scripts/db_init.py has prepared the database.
#   CORS_ORIGINS       the deployed frontend origin, e.g.
#                      https://careerguide-eta.vercel.app
#   AI_PROVIDER etc.   optional. Leave demo until a real key exists; the app
#                      labels every reply as demo-generated either way.
#   GEMINI_API_KEY     or AZURE_SPEECH_KEY. Without one of them a deployed site
#                      cannot speak: the free Edge endpoint answers 403 to
#                      datacenter egress IPs (see scripts/serverless_guard_test.py).
#   RATE_LIMIT_*       optional, and know what they do here: Vercel runs many
#                      short-lived instances and each keeps its own counts, so a
#                      per-instance ceiling is a floor, not a wall. Shared counts
#                      would need a store every instance can reach.
#   AUTH_COOKIE_SECURE the default `auto` is correct for Vercel, whose proxy sends
#                      X-Forwarded-Proto: https, so the cookie is written Secure
#                      and SameSite=None. Note what the two-host layout costs: the
#                      page and the API are separate *.vercel.app hosts, so Safari
#                      and strict-protection Firefox keep no cookie at all and run
#                      on the frontend's in-memory bearer copy instead — which a
#                      reload clears, and the app then re-opens by device id.
#                      Routing /api to this backend from the frontend project, so
#                      both share one host, makes the cookie carry everything.
#
# And in the frontend project: NEXT_PUBLIC_BACKEND_URL = the deployed backend
# origin above, plus CORS_ORIGINS = the deployed frontend origin here. Both sides
# have to name each other: the browser checks the Content-Security-Policy it was
# served (connect-src/media-src carry the backend origin), and FastAPI checks the
# Origin header. A backend that is not in the frontend's CSP is a site where every
# button works and nothing ever loads.
